\subsection{Discrimination of arithmetic sequences}

Figure~\ref{fig:d2-scaling} and Table~\ref{tab:all-seq} present $D_2$
for six arithmetic sequences at $N = 10^5$. The binary prime surface
yields $D_2 = 1.6872 \pm 0.0148$ (bootstrap 95\% CI, $\text{CV} = 0.9\%$),
significantly exceeding the Bernoulli null model
($D_2 = 1.6420 \pm 0.0033$, $z = 13.5$, $p < 10^{-40}$, two-sample
$z$-test against the surrogate ensemble). Sums of two squares
($D_2 = 1.7871$, $z = 7.2$), twin primes ($D_2 = 1.2305$, $z = 3.7$),
and primes in arithmetic progressions modulo~4 ($D_2 \approx 1.548$,
$z \approx 4.2$) all show statistically significant excess $D_2$ above
their respective Bernoulli nulls. The primes $\equiv 1 \pmod 4$ and
$\equiv 3 \pmod 4$ surfaces yield nearly identical $D_2$ values (1.5479
vs.\ 1.5491), indicating that the Chebyshev bias---the preponderance of
primes congruent to 3 modulo 4 over those congruent to 1 modulo 4---does
not manifest in the correlation dimension of the 2D embedding at this
scale.

Crucially, square-free numbers yield
$D_2 = 1.8542$, indistinguishable from their Bernoulli null
($D_2 = 1.8558$, $\Delta = -0.003$, $z = -1.6$). The density of
square-free numbers ($\rho = 0.608$) is much higher than that of
primes ($\rho = 0.096$), yet their $D_2$ is consistent with
independent Bernoulli trials. This negative control demonstrates that
the method does not indiscriminately flag all arithmetic sequences as
structured, but is specifically sensitive to the correlation structure
imposed by primality.

