\subsection{Embedding independence and surrogate validation}

Table~\ref{tab:embeddings} reports $D_2$ for the binary prime surface
under three deterministic embeddings at $N = 50\,000$. In all three
cases, $D_2(\text{prime}) > D_2(\text{Bernoulli})$, with excess
correlation $\Delta D_2$ ranging from $+0.053$ (row-major) to $+0.089$
(Cantor diagonal). The Ulam spiral, which is explicitly designed to
reveal prime structure through diagonal alignments of quadratic
polynomials, yields $\Delta D_2 = +0.074$, intermediate between the
other two embeddings. The persistence of the signal across embeddings
with qualitatively different geometric properties rules out the
possibility that it is an artefact of a particular reshaping convention.

Under random permutation of the 1D$\to$2D mapping (30 surrogates at
$N = 10^5$), $D_2$ drops from $1.6872$ (standard embedding) to
$1.6420 \pm 0.0033$ (surrogate ensemble). The standard embedding value
lies $z = 13.5$ standard deviations above the surrogate mean,
definitively establishing that the $D_2$ signal is intrinsic to the
1D arithmetic sequence and not a product of the embedding geometry
alone. The surrogate ensemble mean ($1.6420$) is itself consistent
with the Bernoulli null ($1.6445$, $z = -0.1$), confirming that the
surrogate procedure effectively destroys the arithmetic correlation
structure.

