           over the matched-density Bernoulli null.}
  \label{tab:embeddings}
  \small
  \begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
    \toprule
    Embedding & $D_2$ (prime) & $D_2$ (Bern.) & $\Delta D_2$ \\
    \midrule
    Row-major & 1.6583 & 1.6052 & +0.0531 \\
    Ulam spiral & 1.6791 & 1.6052 & +0.0739 \\
    Cantor diagonal & 1.6938 & 1.6052 & +0.0886 \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Estimator bias and finite-size effects}

Figure~\ref{fig:multifractal} (left panel) shows the generalised
dimension spectrum $D_q$ for the binary prime surface and the Bernoulli
null at $N = 10^5$. The $D_q$ curves are distinct for all $q$, with the
largest separation at $q \approx 2$. The centre panel shows the
corresponding singularity spectra $f(\alpha)$: the prime surface
exhibits a narrower spectrum ($\Delta\alpha = 0.461$) compared to the
Bernoulli null ($\Delta\alpha = 0.499$). The reduced multifractal width
indicates that the arithmetic constraints imposed by primality
\emph{homogenise} the spatial distribution relative to independent
Bernoulli trials---the surface is less multifractal, not more.

A finite-size analysis of the box-counting estimator applied to
Bernoulli surfaces reveals a substantial bias:
$2 - D_2^{\text{(Bern)}} \approx 12.9/\log N - 0.86$. At $N=10^5$,
this bias accounts for $0.255$ of the observed $2 - D_2$ for the prime
surface ($0.313$). The empirical fit
$D_2^{\text{(prime)}}(N) \approx 2 - 5.10/\log N + 0.15$
($R^2 = 0.967$, $N \in [10^3, 10^7]$) is thus $\sim 253\%$ attributable
to estimator bias rather than to a fundamental scaling law of the prime
distribution. This is a known finite-size effect in box-counting
estimators~\cite{THEILER-1986}: for sparse sets, the number of non-empty
boxes at scale $\varepsilon$ scales as $\varepsilon^{-D_0}$, which for
the binary prime surface ($D_0 \approx 1.94$) grows more slowly than the
embedding would suggest, producing a systematic downward bias in
$\hat{D}_2$ that decays as $1/\log N$.

The bias-cancelled excess $\Delta D_2(N)$ (Eq.~\ref{eq:delta}) is
stable across the tested range: $\Delta D_2 \approx 0.04$--$0.05$ for
$N \in [10^4, 10^7]$. This suggests that while $D_2$ itself is dominated
by estimator bias, the \emph{difference} between the structured and
random surfaces is a robust quantity that may prove more amenable to
theoretical analysis.

\begin{figure*}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_multifractal.pdf}
  \caption{Multifractal analysis of the binary prime surface at
           $N = 10^5$. Left: generalised dimension spectrum $D_q$
           for $q \in [-5, 5]$. Centre: singularity spectrum
           $f(\alpha)$. The prime surface exhibits a narrower spectrum
           ($\Delta\alpha = 0.461$) than the Bernoulli null
           ($\Delta\alpha = 0.499$). Right: 100$\times$100 crop of the
           binary prime surface (black pixels indicate primes).}
  \label{fig:multifractal}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Controlled-correlation ordering}

The $D_2$ values for controlled-correlation surrogate sequences at
matched prime density ($\rho = 0.0959$, $N = 10^5$) establish a
calibrated scale:
\begin{equation}
  \resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{$
    D_2(\mathrm{Block}) = 1.504 \;<\; D_2(\mathrm{Bern}) = 1.645
    \;<\; D_2(\mathrm{Primes}) = 1.687
    \;<\; D_2(\mathrm{Repuls}) = 1.797.
  $}
  \label{eq:ordering}
\end{equation}
The block-clustered sequence, which creates dense patches of ones,
reduces $D_2$ by localising the mass. The repulsive sequence, which
enforces a minimum distance between ones, increases $D_2$ by spreading
the mass more uniformly. Primes occupy an intermediate position closer
to the repulsive than to the clustered extreme, consistent with
anti-clustering arising from the divisibility constraints on prime gaps:
with the sole exception of the pair $(2,3)$, consecutive primes cannot
be adjacent integers; all gaps $p_{n+1} - p_n \geq 2$ are even, and
gaps that are multiples of 6 are over-represented due to the
elimination of numbers divisible by 2 or 3. These constraints suppress
the probability of finding two primes at small 1D separations, which
under the row-major embedding translates into reduced 2D clustering.

\subsection{Secondary results}

The power spectral density (PSD) slope $\alpha$ of the 2D-embedded prime
gap surface ($Z_{ij} = p_{n+1} - p_n$, row-major) was measured at
$N = 10^5$ and $N = 10^6$. Wolf~\cite{WOLF-1997} analysed the 1D
Fourier spectrum of prime gaps and reported $1/f$ noise
($\alpha \approx -1$). In the 2D surface analysis, the PSD slope is
$\alpha = +0.073$ ($N = 10^5$) and $\alpha = -0.054$ ($N = 10^6$),
with $z$-scores against the $1/f$ null model of $z = 37.8$ and
$z = 129.1$, respectively. The 2D-embedded prime gap surface
definitively does \emph{not} exhibit $1/f$ noise; its PSD is consistent
with near-white noise ($\alpha \approx 0$) with a weak anti-correlation
at the largest accessible scale. The deviation from the Wolf spectrum
\emph{increases} with $N$, ruling out convergence to $1/f$ behaviour at
accessible scales and suggesting that the $1/f$ signature reported in
1D may be an artefact of the specific spectral estimator or of the 1D
analysis framework.

The pair-correlation function $g_2(r)$ of $2\,000$ unfolded non-trivial

