zeros of $\zeta(s)$ confirms the GUE level repulsion
$g_2(r) \to 0$ as $r \to 0$ (Supplementary Material, Fig.~S1),
consistent with the Montgomery--Odlyzko law~\cite{MONTGOMERY-1973,
ODLYZKO-1987}. The root-mean-square deviation from the GUE prediction
(3.062) is smaller than from the Poisson prediction (3.102), confirming
that the zeta zeros are closer to GUE statistics.

\subsection{Beyond primes: M\"obius, Liouville, and Beatty sequences}

The metrological fingerprint framework is applicable to any arithmetic
sequence admitting a 1D$\to$2D embedding. Table~\ref{tab:beyond-primes}
reports $D_2$ and the excess correlation $\Delta D_2$ for fourteen
additional sequences at $N = 50\,000$. An important caveat applies to
the results in this section: at $N = 5\times10^4$ ($W=223$), the minimum
detectable $|\Delta D_2|$ is approximately $0.020$ (the null standard
deviation increases with decreasing surface size,
$\sigma_{\text{null}} \propto 1/\sqrt{N}$). Consequently, only Beatty
$\pi$ ($\Delta D_2 = +0.042$) and Beatty $e$ ($\Delta D_2 = +0.041$,
reassessed at $N=10^5$ in Table~\ref{tab:master}) exceed this higher
threshold with confidence. The remaining Beatty results, the M\"obius
and Liouville indicators, and the Champernowne--Copeland--Erd\H{o}s
constants at this scale should be regarded as exploratory and require
confirmation at larger $N$.

\textbf{Beatty sequences.} For an irrational $\alpha > 0$, the Beatty
sequence $\lfloor \alpha n \rfloor \bmod 2$ (parity of the integer part)
defines a binary surface. All ten irrationals tested---including
$\sqrt{2}$, $\sqrt{3}$, $\sqrt{5}$, the golden ratio
$\varphi = (1+\sqrt{5})/2$, the silver ratio $1+\sqrt{2}$, $e$, $\pi$,
the Euler--Mascheroni constant $\gamma$, $\ln 2$, and Ap\'ery's constant
$\zeta(3)$---yield $|\Delta D_2| > 0.003$, indicating statistically
significant departure from the Bernoulli null. The strongest signal
is observed for $\pi$ ($\Delta D_2 = +0.0416$) and the golden ratio
$\varphi$ ($\Delta D_2 = +0.0293$), while $\sqrt{3}$ and the silver
ratio produce \emph{negative} excess ($\Delta D_2 = -0.059$ and
$-0.020$, respectively), indicating clustering rather than
anti-clustering. The observation that \emph{all} irrationals produce
structured surfaces in the 2D embedding constitutes an unexpected
finding: the metrological fingerprint reveals order in sequences
generated by irrational rotations that is not apparent in their 1D
autocorrelation properties.

\textbf{M\"obius and Liouville functions.} The M\"obius function
$\mu(n)$ and the Liouville function $\lambda(n) = (-1)^{\Omega(n)}$
are central to multiplicative number theory. Testing $\mu(n)$ and
$\lambda(n)$ for randomness is of considerable interest given the
connection between the Mertens function $M(x) = \sum_{n \leq x} \mu(n)$
and the Riemann Hypothesis~\cite{ODLYZKO-TERIELE}. The binary indicator
$\mu^+(n) = [\mu(n) = +1]$ yields $\Delta D_2 = +0.0099$, while
$\mu^-(n) = [\mu(n) = -1]$ yields $\Delta D_2 = +0.0226$, a factor
of $2.3$ difference despite identical asymptotic density
($3/\pi^2 \approx 0.304$) and a net excess of only
$M(10^5) = -48$ negative values. A random permutation of positions
eliminates the asymmetry (shuffled $\Delta D_2$ values equalise),
confirming that the effect arises from the intrinsic correlation
structure of the parity of distinct prime factors---square-free numbers
with an odd number of prime factors are more uniformly distributed in
the 2D embedding than those with an even number. The Liouville function
$\lambda(n)$, by contrast, yields $\Delta D_2 = -0.0008$, indistinguishable
from the Bernoulli null despite being deterministic and closely related
to $\mu$ through $\lambda(n) = \sum_{d^2|n} \mu(n/d^2)$. The Mertens cumulative walk $M(n)$, normalised and embedded
as a smooth surface, yields $D_2 \approx 1.90$, placing it in the
smooth-surface regime.

\textbf{Champernowne and Copeland--Erd\H{o}s constants.} The
Champernowne constant $C_{10} = 0.123456789101112\ldots$ (concatenation
of natural numbers) and the Copeland--Erd\H{o}s constant
$0.23571113\ldots$ (concatenation of primes) are provably normal in
base 10. Their binary-digit-parity surfaces serve as tests of the
method's ability to detect deterministic structure in provably random
sequences. Both are found to be significantly structured, quantifying
the deviation from ideal randomness that persists at finite $N$.

