These findings extend the metrological fingerprint concept
beyond the prime sequence. The method detects structure in
deterministically generated sequences (irrational rotations,
multiplicative functions) that standard 1D spectral or correlation
analyses would classify as random, while correctly identifying
the Liouville function as indistinguishable from a Bernoulli

\subsection{Binary digits of fundamental constants}

As a further application of the metrological fingerprint framework,
the binary digit sequences of eight fundamental mathematical constants
were analysed at $N = 5\times 10^4$ digits (Table~\ref{tab:digits}).
The question of whether the digits of $\pi$, $e$, $\sqrt{2}$, and
other constants are statistically normal in base~2---i.e., whether
each binary digit 0 or 1 occurs with asymptotic probability 1/2,
independently---is a major open problem in number
theory~\cite{BAILEY-CRANDALL}. Normality has been proven only for
artificially constructed numbers such as Champernowne's constant
$C_{10} = 0.123456789101112\ldots$ (base~10), while for
$\pi$, $e$, $\sqrt{2}$, and $\zeta(3)$ it remains conjectural.

Seven of the eight constants yield $|\Delta D_2| < 0.013$, consistent
with the null hypothesis of independent, identically distributed
binary digits at this scale. Specifically, $\pi$ ($\Delta D_2 = -0.0022$),
$e$ ($+0.0003$), $\sqrt{2}$ ($+0.0013$), $\sqrt{3}$ ($-0.0015$),
$\sqrt{5}$ ($-0.0001$), $\varphi$ ($-0.0006$), and $\ln 2$ ($+0.0019$)
all fall within the detection threshold. This constitutes the first
metrological test of the normality conjecture for these constants,
and the results are fully consistent with the conjecture at the
accessible scale. The base-2 Champernowne constant
$C_2 = 0.1101110010111011\ldots_2$ (concatenation of binary
representations of integers), which is provably normal in base~2,
yields $\Delta D_2 = +0.0094$, significantly above the threshold.
This is expected: Champernowne's construction is deterministic and
its finite-$N$ digit sequence exhibits systematic deviations from
randomness before the asymptotic limit.

The most striking result is for Ap\'ery's constant $\zeta(3)$
($\Delta D_2 = +0.0042$), which exceeds the detection threshold.
Ap\'ery~\cite{APERY-1979} proved that $\zeta(3)$ is irrational, but
almost nothing is known about the statistical distribution of its
digits in any base. The observed $\Delta D_2(\zeta(3)) > 0$ suggests
that the binary digits of $\zeta(3)$ exhibit anti-clustering---a
detectable departure from independent Bernoulli behaviour---at
$N = 5\times10^4$. To the author's knowledge, this is the first
quantitative evidence for structure in the digit sequence of
$\zeta(3)$.

