\end{table}

The metrological normality test introduced here differs from
traditional statistical tests of randomness (e.g., the
NIST test suite~\cite{NIST-RNG}) in that it probes \emph{spatial}
correlations introduced by the 2D embedding, rather than sequential
or spectral properties in 1D. A constant whose binary digits are
1D-random but exhibit 2D structure would be detected by $D_2$ but
not by standard 1D tests, making the metrological approach a
complementary tool for digit-distribution studies.


process at this scale.

\subsection{Comprehensive metrological fingerprinting}

Table~\ref{tab:master} presents the complete metrological profile
of all fourteen sequences, including the generalised dimensions
$D_0$, $D_1$, $D_2$, and ISO~25178 roughness parameters
$S_a$, $S_q$, $S_{sk}$, $S_{ku}$. The full $D_q$ spectrum
($q \in [-5,5]$) is computed for each sequence, enabling the
classification of scaling behaviour from the capacity dimension
$D_0$ through the correlation dimension $D_2$ to the information
dimension $D_1$.

Several patterns emerge from the comprehensive profile. First,
the \emph{structured} sequences (primes and their arithmetic
sub-classes, sums of two squares, twin primes) consistently
exhibit $D_2 < D_1 < D_0$, the signature of multifractal
scaling. Their skewness $S_{sk}$ is uniformly positive
($+1.2$ to $+8.9$), reflecting the preponderance of empty
space (zero-valued pixels) over occupied space. The kurtosis
$S_{ku}$ ranges from $2.5$ (sums of two squares) to $79.7$
(twin primes), with higher values indicating more extreme
sparsity. Second, the \emph{unstructured} sequences
(square-free, Liouville, M\"obius $\mu^\pm$ at $N=10^5$)
show $D_q \approx 2$ for all $q$, near-zero skewness, and
$S_{ku} \approx 1$--$2$, consistent with their interpretation
as effectively random binary fields at the density and scale
tested.

Third, Beatty sequences are unique in occupying a middle ground:
their $D_0$ and $D_1$ are consistently $\sim 2.1$--$2.2$
(slightly \emph{above} the embedding dimension, an effect
attributable to their density $\rho \approx 0.5$ maximising
the partition-function sensitivity), while their $S_{sk} \approx 0$
and $S_{ku} \approx 1$ reflect the symmetry of the $\bmod 2$
operation. Their $D_2$ values cluster in the range $1.85$--$1.88$,
with $\Delta D_2$ varying in both sign and magnitude depending on
the irrational. Figure~\ref{fig:forest-plot} consolidates all
$\Delta D_2$ results into a single forest plot with 95\% confidence
intervals, Benjamini--Hochberg significance flags, and the
power-analysis detection threshold.

