\subsection{Banach-space descriptors: a complementary fingerprint}

The ISO~25178 framework treats a surface as a geometric object, measuring
height distributions, spatial correlations, and functional bearing-area
properties. An alternative---and mathematically deeper---approach treats
the surface $Z \in \ell^\infty([0,W]^2)$ as an element of a Banach space
and computes functional-analytic invariants: the $L^p$ norms, the
Sobolev seminorm $\|\nabla Z\|_2$, the total variation $\|\nabla Z\|_1$,
and the Banach indicatrix $N(Z, y)$---the number of connected components
at each height level $y$. For binary surfaces ($Z_{ij} \in \{0,1\}$),
these descriptors probe the geometry of the ``prime archipelago'':
$\|\nabla Z\|_2^2$ counts the total length of the coastline separating
zeros from ones (proportional to the number of $0\!\leftrightarrow\!1$
transitions), while $N(Z,1)$ counts the number of distinct islands of
ones.

Table~\ref{tab:banach} and Fig.~\ref{fig:banach} report these
descriptors for six representative surfaces at $N=10^5$, together with
30-ensemble Bernoulli null statistics and $z$-scores. Three findings
emerge. First, the Sobolev seminorm reveals that the prime surface is
significantly \emph{smoother} than the Bernoulli null
($\Delta\|\nabla Z\|_2 = -1.5$, $z = -4.0$): primes exhibit
approximately 50\% fewer $0\!\leftrightarrow\!1$ transitions than
expected under independent Bernoulli trials, a geometric consequence of
the even-gap constraint ($p_{n+1} - p_n \geq 2$ for $p > 2$). This is
the Banach-space counterpart of the anti-clustering signal observed in
$D_2$.

Second, and most importantly, the Banach descriptors and the correlation
dimension $D_2$ are \emph{complementary}: they respond to different
aspects of arithmetic structure. Twin primes show the strongest $D_2$
signal ($\Delta D_2 = +0.0670$, BH-significant) but are
indistinguishable from Bernoulli in the Sobolev seminorm
($\Delta\|\nabla Z\|_2 = +0.4$, $z = +0.9$, n.s.). Conversely, the
Beatty $\varphi$ surface produces a staggering Banach signal
($\Delta\|\nabla Z\|_2 = +26.1$, $z = +164$)---reflecting the geometric
Moir\'e stripe pattern---but only a modest $D_2$ excess
($\Delta D_2 = +0.020$). The Pearson correlation between the H$^1$
excess and $\Delta D_2$ across the six sequences is $r = -0.31$,
confirming that the two descriptor families capture largely orthogonal
information. This complementarity is a strength of the overall
framework: the metrological fingerprint ($D_2$) is sensitive to subtle
arithmetic correlations (twin primes), while the Banach fingerprint
($\|\nabla Z\|_2$, TV) is sensitive to geometric texture (Moir\'e
patterns), and the two together provide a richer characterisation than
either alone.

Third, square-free numbers again serve as a discriminating negative
control: their H$^1$ seminorm is dramatically lower than Bernoulli
($\Delta\|\nabla Z\|_2 = -11.4$, $z = -59.5$), reflecting the fact that
the square-free indicator transitions are suppressed by the high density
($\rho = 0.608$) combined with structured gaps. The M\"obius $\mu^-$
indicator, while metrologically transparent ($\Delta D_2 = -0.002$,
n.s.), is strongly flagged by the Banach descriptors
($\Delta\|\nabla Z\|_2 = -2.9$, $z = -10.1$), demonstrating that the
Banach fingerprint can detect structure in sequences to which $D_2$ is
blind---consistent with the concept of ``metrologically transparent''
sequences introduced in Section~4.4.

