\subsection{Convergence of $\Delta D_2(N)$}

Figure~\ref{fig:convergence} and Table~\ref{tab:convergence} report
$\Delta D_2(N)$ for four representative sequences across
$N \in [5\times10^3, 10^6]$. The prime surface exhibits a
persistent positive $\Delta D_2$ at all scales, decreasing
gradually from $+0.048$ at $N=5\times10^3$ to $+0.022$ at
$N=10^6$. This persistence across three orders of magnitude
establishes $\Delta D_2$(primes) $>0$ as a robust,
scale-invariant feature of the prime distribution in 2D.

The M\"obius $\mu^-$ surface shows a different behaviour:
$\Delta D_2$ fluctuates between $+0.028$ and $-0.016$ with no
consistent sign, and its magnitude at $N=10^5$ ($-0.002$) falls
below the detection threshold ($|\Delta D_2| < 0.013$). The earlier
report of M\"obius asymmetry (Section~3.5) at $N=5\times10^4$ is
thus a finite-$N$ fluctuation, not a persistent structural feature.
This illustrates the importance of multi-scale validation before
claiming structural signatures.

The Beatty $\varphi$ surface exhibits a striking oscillatory
behaviour: $\Delta D_2$ alternates between $+0.039$ and $-0.057$,
with a periodicity related to the alignment between the
quasi-period $q_k$ (the $k$-th convergent denominator of $\varphi$)
and the grid width $W = \lfloor\sqrt{N}\rfloor$. When
$q_k \approx W$ (e.g., $q_{10} = 89$ at $W=70$, or
$q_{12} = 233$ at $W=223$), the Moir\'e pattern aligns with
the grid diagonal, maximising $|\Delta D_2|$. When $q_k$ and $W$
are incommensurate, the pattern averages out and
$|\Delta D_2| \to 0$. This geometric resonance confirms the
Moir\'e interpretation of Beatty surface structure and provides
a quantitative test: the $N$ values yielding extreme
$|\Delta D_2|$ should correspond to Fibonacci numbers (for
$\varphi$) or Pell numbers (for $\sqrt{2}$).

\begin{figure}[htbp]
