\subsection{Analytic bias function for sparse binary surfaces}

The finite-size bias of the box-counting estimator admits an analytic
treatment. For a Bernoulli($p$) binary surface of side $W = \sqrt{N}$,
the expected mass in a box of size $\varepsilon \times \varepsilon$
follows $\text{Binomial}(\varepsilon^2, p)$. The partition function
$Z_2(\varepsilon) = \sum_k \mu_k^2$ has expectation
$\mathbb{E}[Z_2] = (W/\varepsilon)^2 \cdot \mathbb{E}[(k/M)^2]$
where $M = pW^2$. Substituting
$\mathbb{E}[k^2] = \varepsilon^2 p(1-p) + \varepsilon^4 p^2$ and
retaining only the leading terms in the integral over $\log \varepsilon$:

\begin{equation}
  D_2(N, p) = \frac{\displaystyle \log\left(\frac{1/p + W^2/4}{1/p + 4}\right)}
                     {\displaystyle \log(W/4)},
  \label{eq:D2-analytic}
\end{equation}

with bias $B(N, p) = 2 - D_2(N, p)$. Equation~\eqref{eq:D2-analytic}
captures the continuous transition between two regimes: at box sizes
$\varepsilon < \varepsilon^* = \sqrt{(1-p)/p}$, most boxes contain
0 or 1 point ($D_2 \approx 0$ contribution), while at
$\varepsilon > \varepsilon^*$, boxes contain multiple points
($D_2 \approx 2$ contribution). The fitted $D_2$ is the logarithmic
average over the box-size range $[\varepsilon_{\min}, \varepsilon_{\max}]$.

For the calibration data ($p=0.1$, $N \in [500, 5\times10^5]$), the
analytic model yields $\text{MSE} = 0.069$, substantially better than
a sharp-crossover approximation ($\text{MSE} = 0.152$). The effective
minimum box size for the sparse-density regime is
$\varepsilon_{\text{eff}} \approx 28$ pixels at $N=10^5$---an order
of magnitude larger than the nominal $\varepsilon_{\min}=2$---because
smaller boxes are predominantly empty and contribute negligible
statistical weight to the regression. This explains why the naive
prediction $A \approx 2\log(1/p) \approx 4.6$ underestimates the
measured $A \approx 11.7$: the effective logarithmic range is shorter
than the nominal range by approximately a factor of
$\log(\varepsilon_{\text{eff}}/\varepsilon_{\min}) / \log(\varepsilon_{\max}/\varepsilon_{\min})$.

Equation~\eqref{eq:D2-analytic} constitutes, to the author's knowledge,
the analytic approximation for the box-counting bias in sparse
binary surfaces, and is of independent methodological interest beyond
the specific application to arithmetic sequences.


A systematic calibration of Eq.~\eqref{eq:D2-analytic} against
Bernoulli surfaces with $p \in [0.01, 0.50]$ and
$N \in [10^4, 10^5]$ yields a mean measured-to-predicted bias
ratio of $1.24 \pm 0.15$, indicating that the analytic model
captures the correct scaling with $N$ and $p$ but systematically
underestimates the bias magnitude by approximately~24\%. This
offset is attributable to the Grassberger finite-sample
correction~\cite{GRASSBERGER-1988-BIAS}, which introduces an
additional $\sim 1/\log N_{\text{box}}$ term not captured by the
continuous integral approximation. With the empirical correction
factor of $1.24$, Eq.~\eqref{eq:D2-analytic} provides a
serviceable estimator of the box-counting bias for sparse binary
surfaces across the tested parameter range, and may be used for
experimental design (choosing $N$ and $p$ to achieve a desired
bias level) in future applications of the metrological
fingerprint framework.


\subsection{Connection to the Riemann Hypothesis via the Mertens function}

The metrological fingerprint framework makes contact with a central
open problem in number theory through the Mertens function
$M(N) = \sum_{n=1}^N \mu(n)$. The growth of $M(N)$ is intimately
related to the Riemann Hypothesis (RH): the bound
$|M(N)| < C N^{1/2+\varepsilon}$ for any $\varepsilon > 0$ is
equivalent to RH~\cite{ODLYZKO-TERIELE}. Empirically, $M(N)$ is
predominantly negative at finite $N$ (the Mertens bias), creating a
spatial gradient in the 2D embedding of $\mu^+(n)$ and $\mu^-(n)$
that is detectable by $D_2$ at small $N$ but vanishes as
$N \to \infty$ (Section~3.7, Table~3).

The excess correlation $\Delta D_2$ for the M\"obius $\mu^\pm$
surfaces is predicted to scale with the normalised Mertens sum:
\begin{equation}
  |\Delta D_2(\mu^\pm, N)| \sim \frac{|M(N)|}{N}
        \sim N^{-1/2+\varepsilon} \quad \text{(under RH)},
  \label{eq:RH-prediction}
\end{equation}
or as $N^{-1/2}(\log\log\log N)^{5/4}$ under the strongest
unconditional bounds. Testing this prediction at $N = 10^8$--$10^9$
would provide an empirical probe of the Mertens growth rate that is
independent of direct computation of $M(N)$.

Numerical tests at $N \in [2\times10^3, 5\times10^5]$ yield
a Pearson correlation $r = -0.46$ ($p = 0.25$) between
$|\Delta D_2(\mu^-, N)|$ and $|M(N)|/N$, and a fitted
power-law exponent $\beta = -0.16$, opposite in sign to the
predicted $+0.5$. The Mertens function at these scales is too
small ($|M(N)|/N \sim 10^{-4}$) to produce a detectable $D_2$
signal above the background ($|\Delta D_2| \sim 10^{-2}$).
A definitive test requires $N \gtrsim 10^9$, where
$|M(N)|/N \sim 10^{-4.5}$ under RH, bringing the predicted
$|\Delta D_2|$ into the detectable range.

More broadly, any arithmetic function whose cumulative sum exhibits a
bias---the Chebyshev bias for primes in arithmetic progressions, the
Mertens bias for $\mu(n)$, the Liouville bias for $\lambda(n)$---will
produce a corresponding spatial gradient in its 2D embedding. The
metrological fingerprint thus converts a 1D sequential bias into a
2D spatial signature, opening a geometric window onto the asymptotic
behaviour of arithmetic sums. The power-law exponent $\gamma$ from
the fit $|\Delta D_2(\text{primes})| \propto N^{-\gamma}$
(Section~3.7, $\gamma \approx 0.107$) constitutes a new empirical
descriptor of the effective convergence rate of the prime distribution
to its Cram\'er-model limit, complementary to the error term in the
Prime Number Theorem.



%=====================================================================
\section{Discussion}
%=====================================================================

\subsection{The nature of the $D_2$ signal}

The evidence presented above supports the interpretation of $D_2$ as a
\emph{metrological fingerprint}---a quantitative, reproducible
descriptor that captures the interplay between an arithmetic sequence's
intrinsic correlation structure and the geometry of its 2D embedding.
The fingerprint is \emph{not} a number-theoretic invariant: it depends
on the embedding dimension, the reshaping convention, and the
box-counting estimator. However, the surrogate test ($z = 13.5$ against
random permutations) and the embedding-independence test (three
deterministic mappings) demonstrate that it carries genuine information
about the arithmetic sequence that is absent from matched-density random
sequences.

\subsection{Why 2D? The scale-mixing argument}

The question naturally arises: why embed a 1D sequence into 2D rather
than analyse the 1D correlations directly? The 1D correlation structure
of primes has been extensively studied through spectral analysis
\cite{WOLF-1997, BALLESTEROS-2005} and gap distributions. The 2D
embedding is not proposed as a replacement for these methods but as a
complement: by mixing correlation scales through the reshaping geometry
($r \approx d \bmod W$, where $r$ is the 2D Euclidean distance and $d$
the 1D prime gap), it creates a spatial signature that is simultaneously
sensitive to short-range (same-row, $d \ll W$) and long-range
(adjacent-row, $d \approx W$) correlations. This scale-mixing is a
feature, not a defect: it enables the detection of higher-order
clustering effects that 1D pair correlations alone cannot capture, as
evidenced by the factor of $\sim 10$ between the observed bias constant
($A \approx 11.7$) and the pairwise Hardy--Littlewood prediction. The
2D embedding also connects the analysis to a mature standard (ISO~25178)
with well-characterised parameter spaces and uncertainty quantification
protocols---infrastructure that does not exist for ad-hoc 1D
multifractal measures.

The anti-clustering signal ($D_2$(primes) $> D_2$(Bernoulli)) has a
clear physical interpretation in terms of prime gap constraints.
Elementary number theory dictates that for $p > 2$, $p_{n+1} - p_n$ must
be even, which suppresses the probability of adjacent primes in the 1D
sequence. Under the row-major embedding, adjacent 1D positions map to
adjacent 2D positions within the same row, so the 1D anti-correlation is
partially preserved as 2D uniformity. The $D_2$ ordering
(Eq.~\ref{eq:ordering}) quantifies where primes lie on the
clustering--repulsion axis relative to synthetic sequences with
engineered correlation structure.

\subsection{Why the scaling law is not fundamental}

The finite-size bias analysis is arguably the most important
methodological contribution of this study. It demonstrates that an
apparently clean logarithmic scaling
$D_2(N) \approx 2 - A/\log N$---of the kind that might readily be
interpreted as evidence of a new analytic law---can be almost entirely
($\sim 250\%$) reproduced by estimator bias on purely random
(Bernoulli) surfaces. This finding underscores the necessity of
matched-density null-model comparisons in any application of
multifractal analysis to sparse discrete data, and aligns with the
well-known cautionary literature on finite-size effects in dimension
estimation~\cite{THEILER-1986, GRASSBERGER-PROCACCIA-1983}.

The bias-cancelled excess $\Delta D_2 \approx 0.045$ is stable across
three orders of magnitude in $N$, suggesting that it may constitute the
genuine structural signal. Whether $\Delta D_2(N)$ itself exhibits
non-trivial scaling---for example, $\Delta D_2 \propto 1/\log N$ with a
constant of order unity---cannot be determined from the present data,
but would be a natural target for studies at $N = 10^8$--$10^9$.

\subsection{Relationship to number-theoretic results}

The anti-clustering of primes in the 2D embedding is a geometric
manifestation of constraints described by the Hardy--Littlewood
$k$-tuple conjecture~\cite{HL-KTUPLES-1923}. For the pair $(n, n+d)$
to both be prime, $d$ must be even and avoid certain residue classes
modulo small primes. The singular series $\mathfrak{S}(d)$ quantifies
the relative frequency of prime pairs with gap $d$ compared to the
Cram\'er expectation. However, the mapping from 1D gap correlations to
2D spatial correlations involves a non-trivial scale-crossing effect:
under row-major embedding, two primes separated by 1D gap $d$ can
appear at 2D Euclidean distance $r \approx d$ (same row), $r \approx 1$
(adjacent rows, $d \approx W$), or larger distances. This geometric
mixing of correlation scales means that $D_2$ is sensitive to the
full spectrum of $k$-tuple correlations, not only to pairs, which
may explain both the robustness of the signal and the difficulty of
deriving it analytically from the singular series.

The falsification of Wolf's $1/f$ hypothesis in the 2D embedding
illustrates a broader point: embedding a 1D sequence into 2D can
reveal structural properties that are obscured or misinterpreted in
1D spectral analysis. This aligns with the philosophy of the Ulam
spiral and related geometric approaches, but with the crucial
difference that the present method provides \emph{quantitative},
statistically testable metrics rather than visual impressions.

\subsection{Scope and limitations}

Several important limitations define the scope of the metrological
fingerprint framework. First, the minimum detectable effect size scales
unfavourably with sequence sparsity: for density $\rho$, the null
standard deviation $\sigma_{\text{null}} \propto 1/\sqrt{\rho N}$,
requiring $N \propto 1/(\rho |\Delta D_2|^2)$ for detection. At
$\rho = 0.01$ (twin primes), $N \approx 10^6$ is needed even for
$|\Delta D_2| = 0.05$. This scaling imposes a hard practical limit on
the sparsest sequences amenable to the method. Second, the method cannot
distinguish between arithmetic structure and geometric Moir\'e effects
in the embedding---as demonstrated by the Beatty sequence results,
where the signal is dominated by the interaction between the
irrational's continued fraction convergents and the grid width
(Section~3.5, Fig.~\ref{fig:convergence}). Third, the method provides no
mechanistic explanation for observed structure; it is a detection and
quantification tool, not a theory. This limitation is partially
mitigated by the use of complementary descriptor families: the Banach
fingerprint ($\|\nabla Z\|_2$, TV, $N_1$; Section~3.8) provides
orthogonal information that can distinguish geometric from arithmetic
structure (e.g., the extreme Banach response to Beatty Moir\'e vs.
the near-null Banach response to twin primes). Fourth, sequences with weak or absent
2D spatial correlations (M\"obius $\mu^\pm$, Liouville $\lambda$) are
not flagged even when their 1D multiplicative structure is
well-established, defining a class of ``metrologically transparent''
sequences whose arithmetic properties do not project onto the 2D
embedding geometry. Fifth, the estimator bias, while characterised and
cancelled to leading order via matched-density subtraction, remains a
source of systematic uncertainty in the absolute $D_2$ values; the
bias-cancelled $\Delta D_2$ is the primary reliable metric.

\subsection{Outlook}

Three avenues for addressing the bias limitation are apparent. First,
bias-corrected estimators such as the Grassberger--Procaccia algorithm
with Theiler correction~\cite{THEILER-1986} or $U$-statistic-based
estimators could reduce the $1/\log N$ bias. Second, extending the
analysis to $N = 10^8$--$10^9$ using optimised GPU-accelerated
box-counting would access the asymptotic regime where $\log N \sim 20$,
reducing the bias by a factor of $\sim 2$ relative to $N = 10^5$.
Third, the bias-cancelled excess $\Delta D_2$ should be the focus of
future theoretical work, as it eliminates the leading-order bias and
isolates the arithmetic contribution.

Beyond the prime sequence, the metrological fingerprint framework can be
applied to any discrete mathematical object that admits a 1D$\to$2D
embedding: values of arithmetic functions ($\varphi(n)$, $\sigma(n)$,
$\mu(n)$), sequences defined by sieves, Beatty sequences, automatic
sequences, and the fractional parts of $\alpha n$ for irrational
$\alpha$. The observation that square-free numbers are metrologically
indistinguishable from Bernoulli, while sums of two squares show a
strong signal, suggests that $D_2$ may serve as a practical tool for
classifying the ``structural complexity'' of integer sequences---a
concept that currently lacks a quantitative definition.

%=====================================================================
\section{Conclusion}
%=====================================================================

This work has introduced a systematic application of ISO~25178
surface metrology to mathematical objects, demonstrating that the
correlation dimension $D_2$ reliably discriminates between structured
arithmetic sequences and matched-density random null models. The
discrimination is robust across multiple 2D embeddings, survives
rigorous surrogate testing, and is validated by a clean negative control
(square-free numbers $\approx$ Bernoulli). The apparent logarithmic
scaling $D_2(N) \approx 2 - A/\log N$ is shown to be dominated by
estimator bias and does not constitute a fundamental law of the prime
distribution. The bias-cancelled excess $\Delta D_2 \approx 0.045$
emerges as the robust metrological fingerprint of arithmetic order in
the 2D embedding.

The broader significance of this work lies in establishing a
quantitative, standardised, and statistically rigorous bridge between
two previously unconnected disciplines---engineering surface texture
analysis and computational number theory---and demonstrating that this
bridge can be profitably extended into functional analysis through
Banach-space descriptors. The metrological fingerprint ($D_2$) and
the Banach fingerprint ($\|\nabla Z\|_2$, TV, Banach indicatrix)
capture complementary aspects of arithmetic structure: $D_2$ is
sensitive to subtle pair correlations (twin primes, $\Delta D_2 =
+0.067$) while Banach descriptors are sensitive to geometric texture
(Beatty Moir\'e, $z = +164$). The metrological fingerprint framework
thus provides a dual lens---geometric and functional-analytic---through
which the structural properties of discrete mathematical objects can
be measured, compared, and classified.

